WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.442 --> 00:00:02.400
SPEAKER 1: In Google
Cloud Engineering Support,

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.900
our goal is to work smoothly
with you, engineer to engineer,

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.530
to resolve any issues you may
encounter when using Google

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.570
Cloud Platform products.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.430
This video describes
best practices

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.870
for filing an issue report
with our support engineers

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:15.630
in the Google Cloud
Support Center,

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.970
but it's applicable to
requesting technical assistance

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.860
from any GCP engineer.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.130
This includes support cases,
bug reports, and issue trackers,

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:26.520
and posts to our user groups
and forums, like Stack Overflow.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.170
The key principle is clarity.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:30.240
Specify the right level
of technical detail

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.610
and communicate your
expectations explicitly

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:35.220
so that we can better help
you resolve the issue.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.320
The Support Center's help
page and earlier videos

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.382
present the mechanics
of how to file cases.

00:00:39.382 --> 00:00:41.840
This will help you understand
what to include in your cases

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:42.690
and why.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:44.210
We will explain
the details we need

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:45.776
and why they are important.

00:00:45.776 --> 00:00:48.540
The four critical details
to include with every case

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.930
are specific times when you
experience the issue, the GCP

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.290
products you're
using, the location,

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:56.400
and specific identifiers
for any resources

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.790
relevant to your case.

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:59.670
Onset time and
duration give us a way

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:01.350
to focus our time
series monitoring

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:02.820
on the relevant period.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.160
Please be explicit about
whether the issue is ongoing

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.470
or whether it was only
observable in the past.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.600
If the issue isn't
ongoing, please state that,

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.640
and give the end
time if you know it.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.370
ISO 8601 format is a
good choice because it's

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.380
unambiguous and easy to sort.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.162
Please always include
the time zone.

00:01:18.162 --> 00:01:20.370
Our internal systems tend
to be in Google time, which

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.160
is US-specific, but our agents
use a follow the sun model

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.470
and may be in
different time zones.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.492
Some issues are
intermittent over a period.

00:01:27.492 --> 00:01:28.950
In this case, it's
good to say when

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.350
it's first observed or perhaps
note a time in the past

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:32.880
when it was not happening.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.980
Remark about the
frequency of observations

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.440
and note one or two
specific examples.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.580
When time is specified
in a relative way,

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.160
including omission
of a time zone,

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.310
the reader must convert
into an absolute format

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:46.260
that can be input into time
series monitoring tools.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.300
This is error-prone
and costly, especially

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.536
considering that each
reader must pay the price.

00:01:50.536 --> 00:01:52.410
For example, sending an
email about something

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.870
that happened earlier
yesterday causes the reader

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.820
to search for a header
in the initial email

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.050
and start doing
math in their head.

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.630
Phrases to avoid are things
like earlier today, because it's

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.210
not specific enough, or
yesterday, because we need

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:06.190
to figure out the
implied date when issues

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:08.110
take multiple days to resolve.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.389
Don't use dates like
9/8 because it's

00:02:10.389 --> 00:02:13.950
ambiguous and may be interpreted
differently in other locales.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:16.880
The Case Creation form includes
basic product selection,

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.540
which is used for routing
to a specialist in the area.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.070
This isn't specific
enough to locate

00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:24.250
the components or logs that
will help diagnose the issue.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.350
Be as specific as
possible about the product

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:29.190
you're using, ideally
referring to the specific APIs

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:33.160
or console.cloud.google.com
or screenshots.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:35.170
For APIs, you can link
to the documentation

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:38.224
page, which contains the
product name and the URL.

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:39.640
Tell us about the
mechanism you're

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:41.440
using to initiate the request.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:45.740
For example, REST API, gcloud
tool, console.cloud.google.com,

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.042
or perhaps a tool like
Deployment Manager.

00:02:48.042 --> 00:02:49.750
Sometimes multiple
products are involved,

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.060
so try to be specific
about each one.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.730
Describe the behavior observing
and what you expected,

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.067
then show us what you see.

00:02:57.067 --> 00:02:59.400
You should be specific about
the actions you have taken,

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.980
as well as the syntax
of any commands you use.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:03.900
A phrase like "can't
create instance"

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.210
doesn't tell us whether
you used gcloud compute,

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:09.904
the developer console UI, or
called it from your own code.

00:03:09.904 --> 00:03:12.070
The most clear way to
communicate this example would

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:15.340
be something like "I ran
gcloud compute instances create

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:19.120
my-instance--zone us-central1-a
and got the following error

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.647
message."

00:03:20.647 --> 00:03:22.480
When including the
location, region and zone

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.430
are important to us,
because we often roll out

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:26.435
changes to one region
or zone at a time.

00:03:26.435 --> 00:03:28.810
They are a proxy for the
version number of the underlying

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:29.791
software.

00:03:29.791 --> 00:03:32.290
We can see if there's a rollout
underway in a given location

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:35.407
or map it to an internal release
ID for use in a bug report.

00:03:35.407 --> 00:03:37.240
You should include
something along the lines

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:41.380
of "I tried regions
us-east1 or us-central1."

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:43.480
In your case, please make
sure to always include

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:45.271
specific identifiers.

00:03:45.271 --> 00:03:47.770
Project ID is an input to most
of our troubleshooting tools,

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:50.440
and it's a required
field when filing a case.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:53.680
Please provide a numeric
project ID, not a project name.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.570
If the error is observed
in multiple projects

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:57.370
or in one project,
but not another,

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:59.920
you should include that
information in the description.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:02.360
Also include IDs of other
objects, like instance

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.630
IDs, BigQuery job
IDs, or table names.

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:08.920
IP addresses are another form
of unambiguous identifier.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.170
When specifying a
cloud platform IP,

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:13.150
tell us the context
of how it's used--

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:15.800
for example, if it's
connected to a GCE instance,

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.835
a load balancer, a custom route,
or if it's an API endpoint.

00:04:18.835 --> 00:04:20.709
If you're troubleshooting
a connection issue,

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:22.480
then tell us a bit
about the IP addresses

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:23.874
that are not part of GCP--

00:04:23.874 --> 00:04:25.540
for example, if it's
your home internet,

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:29.526
a VPN end point, or an
external monitoring system.

00:04:29.526 --> 00:04:31.650
You should always provide
more specific information

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:33.600
than something like
"one of our instances"

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:35.430
or "we can't connect
from the internet,"

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:36.888
because support
will need to gather

00:04:36.888 --> 00:04:39.750
more information from you before
they can begin troubleshooting.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.510
That will mean further delays
in resolving your issue.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:44.550
For any information
you provide to support,

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.230
be specific and explicit.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:49.740
If relevant, use a screenshot
to show exactly what you see.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.470
For web-based interfaces,
provide a .HAR file or a HTTP

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:53.320
archive.

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.320
Our documentation has
instructions on how to get

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.050
a .HAR file from
three major browsers.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:00.930
When troubleshooting networking
issues, attach TCP dump output

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.900
if you collect it, and also
attach log snippets and example

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:07.170
stack traces you believe to
be relevant to the issue.

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:09.680
When you file a case, support
uses the priority field

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:13.039
for initial routing, especially
when an issue may page someone.

00:05:13.039 --> 00:05:15.330
Case priorities are also
described in the case creation

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:16.470
form itself.

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:19.020
Use P1 for production
emergencies.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.804
Think of case priority in terms
of the impact to your business.

00:05:21.804 --> 00:05:23.970
In addition to setting the
priority field correctly,

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:25.636
it's useful to add a
sentence describing

00:05:25.636 --> 00:05:27.381
the impact in your words.

00:05:27.381 --> 00:05:29.880
For example, you may consider
a problem with the dev version

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.120
to be a P1, even if no end users
are directly impacted, if it's

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.490
blocking a critical
security fix.

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:36.990
To avoid incorrect
assumptions, it's

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:40.350
helpful to be explicit
about why you selected P1.

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:42.181
Cases have built-in
response timers,

00:05:42.181 --> 00:05:44.430
which are designed to set
reasonable expectations most

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:45.484
of the time.

00:05:45.484 --> 00:05:47.400
If you have some specific
needs, please let us

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:48.825
know when you need an update.

00:05:48.825 --> 00:05:51.450
It's good to say something like,
"I need a response by 5:00 PM,

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:53.467
because that's when
my shift ends."

00:05:53.467 --> 00:05:55.800
If you notice you're affected
by an issue that's already

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.185
reported on the
status.cloud.google.com,

00:05:58.185 --> 00:06:00.780
you can follow the progress
in the dashboard itself

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:03.000
or use the Me Too link in
the cloud support portal

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:06.256
to automatically get
updates for known issues.

00:06:06.256 --> 00:06:08.380
When an issue needs to be
handled around the clock,

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:10.600
customers with 24/7
support can request

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:12.174
that the case follow the sun.

00:06:12.174 --> 00:06:14.590
Cases that follow the sun will
be reassigned several times

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:17.770
per day, so it's always with
an active support engineer.

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:20.300
A lower priority case will not
be managed around the clock,

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.383
as the assigned support
engineer will go off shift

00:06:22.383 --> 00:06:23.392
at some point.

00:06:23.392 --> 00:06:25.600
This can cause delays if
the assigned engineer is not

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:26.592
in your time zone.

00:06:26.592 --> 00:06:28.800
You can ask us to manage
the ticket in your time zone

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.925
so you can communicate more
easily with the support

00:06:30.925 --> 00:06:31.690
engineer.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:34.000
For example, you can say
"please manage this case

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:35.640
in my time zone, EST."

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:38.140
This can help if you need a
long discussion with the support

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:40.014
engineer, but may not
help if the development

00:06:40.014 --> 00:06:42.070
team is in another time zone.

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:44.500
If you need to continue the
conversation over Google

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:46.780
Hangouts or phone
after filing a case,

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:48.682
please provide a
link or phone number.

00:06:48.682 --> 00:06:50.140
If you request it,
support will try

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:51.556
to call when it
does not interfere

00:06:51.556 --> 00:06:52.810
with issue resolution.

00:06:52.810 --> 00:06:55.529
For video bridges, we strongly
prefer Google Hangouts

00:06:55.529 --> 00:06:57.820
but can use anything that
works within a Chrome browser

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:00.340
without requiring extensions.

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:02.500
If you're not requesting
an immediate callback,

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:03.970
please give two or
three time slots

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:07.130
when you're available to get
started with the scheduling.

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:08.210
Thank you for watching.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:10.543
If you have questions, please
leave them in the comments

00:07:10.543 --> 00:07:13.590
section below, and we'll try to
address them in future videos.

